# Cline Rules

## Cache Optimization

- Always put stable content first (rules, file summaries, project context), dynamic content last — this keeps the cache prefix intact and maximizes DeepSeek cache hits.
- Never add timestamps, task IDs, or session metadata to prompts — they invalidate the cache prefix on every request.
- Prefer replace_in_file over full file reads — sending only the affected block keeps input tokens low and the stable prefix unchanged.
- Read each file once per task and reference it; never re-read the same file unnecessarily.
- Skip environment_details and directory trees unless the current task explicitly requires them.
- Avoid /compact mid-task — only suggest it when switching to a completely separate task.

## File Editing

- replace_in_file for all edits — full rewrites only if explicitly asked.
- Files over 100 lines: edit only the affected block.

## Coding Behavior

- Modify only what is required.
- Reuse existing patterns and architecture.
- Never expose API keys in frontend code.

## Communication

- Concise and direct. No restating requests or summarizing actions.
- Text-only response if no changes requested.
- Max one clarifying question per reply.

## Task Planning

- 3+ files or unclear scope: ask all questions before writing any code.

## Vue / Ionic / Firebase

- Composition API only, matching existing project patterns.
- Incremental UI edits — no full redesigns.

## Email Templates

- Hex colors only — no rgba() in main backgrounds.
- Hex colors only — no rgba() in main backgrounds.

## Context Management

- Over 50 tool calls: suggest new chat.

## Finishing Work

-After finishing the response for user (if it requires, run npm run dev) so the user doesn´t need to do it by himself
